 "drivingQuestion": "What was the great repair?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "R. Elazar identifies the repair from another mishna",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sukkah.51b.10",
   "daf": "51b",
   "sentenceId": 9,
   "he": "בְּמוֹצָאֵי יוֹם טוֹב כּוּ׳. מַאי תִּיקּוּן גָּדוֹל? אָמַר רַבִּי אֶלְעָזָר: כְּאוֹתָהּ שֶׁשָּׁנִינוּ: חֲלָקָה הָיְתָה בָּרִאשׁוֹנָה. וְהִקִּיפוּהָ גְּזוּזְטְרָא, וְהִתְקִינוּ שֶׁיְּהוּ נָשִׁים יוֹשְׁבוֹת מִלְמַעְלָה וַאֲנָשִׁים מִלְּמַטָּה.",
   "en": "§ The mishna continues: «At the conclusion of» the first «Festival» day, etc., the priests and the Levites descended from the Israelites’ courtyard to the Women’s Courtyard, where they would introduce a significant repair. The Gemara asks: «What» is this «significant repair? Rabbi Elazar said» that «it is like that which we learned:» The walls of the Women’s Courtyard «were smooth,» without protrusions, «initially.» Subsequently, they affixed protrusions to the wall surrounding the Women’s Courtyard. Each year thereafter, for the Celebration of the Place of the Drawing of the Water, they placed wooden planks on these projections and «surrounded» the courtyard «with a balcony [gezuztra]. And they instituted that» the «women should sit above and» the «men below.»"
  }
 ],
